{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"proposed-kitschy-firefly","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / problem\n\nA fresh single-branch clone of the tickets store shows recurring `.bridge_state/peer_confirmations.json` churn even when the authoritative peer link set is unchanged. The current file contains 3,499 records and is 1,400,452 bytes. Git history contains 418 commits touching it and 417 unique blobs totaling 320,864,708 raw bytes. Since the tickets-history rewrite, this path alone contributes 51,853,550 packed-entry bytes.\n\nThe latest observed reconcile pass changed 1,225 existing records solely in `confirmed_pass` and `confirmed_at`; no confirmation identity or decision input changed. `PeerConfirmationStore.record()` overwrites those fields and unconditionally sets `_dirty=True` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/peer_confirmations.py:142-173`), and `save()` then rewrites the full JSON sidecar (`:175-189`). Repository-wide search finds no production reader of either volatile field outside this writer; only tests assert them. A two-pass isolated runtime probe over the same authoritative snapshot wrote the file twice and produced different hashes solely because the pass/time fields refreshed.\n\nThis is separate from the principal checkout-size incident: it materially grows reachable Git history, but it does not explain the >1 GB working tree. It recreates the same no-op bridge-state churn class previously addressed for other binding fields.\n\n## Origins / design constraints\n\nThe evidence store was introduced by `nonlineal-melancholy-grouper` (`248f-ac00-18db-4259`) and snapshot reconfirmation by `soured-elmy-ostrich` (`83d3-61d9-fc59-424b`). The safety invariant must remain: an observed link is durable positive evidence and absence never un-confirms it. Any repair must decide explicitly whether last-seen pass/time are a supported audit contract or removable telemetry; silently weakening confirmation safety is out of scope.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Establish the intended consumer contract for `confirmed_pass` and `confirmed_at`.\n- Add a RED two-pass oracle over an unchanged authoritative snapshot that proves the chosen no-op persistence behavior while preserving confirmation safety and provenance.\n- Make persistence content-aware or redesign the volatile telemetry so unchanged link evidence does not rewrite the full sidecar each pass.\n- Measure resulting blob/pack churn and document any migration or compatibility implications.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Two identical authoritative snapshots do not create a second full-file content change solely because pass/time advanced, or the approved alternative demonstrably bounds equivalent Git growth.\n- [ ] Link confirmation remains relation-scoped, monotonic, durable across compaction, and fail-open exactly as documented.\n- [ ] The supported contract for last-confirmed pass/time is explicit and has a production consumer or is removed/migrated deliberately.\n- [ ] A regression test exercises two complete persistence passes and compares the on-disk artifact.\n- [ ] Before/after evidence reports unique blob and packed-byte growth for a representative unchanged pass.","id":"266c-394a-9e6f-4c2e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["reconciler","storage","git-churn"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Peer-confirmation refresh rewrites the full sidecar on every reconcile pass"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787517813871446001,"uuid":"b858d819-387b-46f3-8c12-f0833b5470e0"}